Conclusions
The prompt works. Across three model families and five configurations, every secured output was meaningfully more secure than its unsecured counterpart. The effect was consistent, not stochastic.

No model produced secure code by default. Every unsecured output contained at least the innerHTML/XSS vulnerability, unprotected storage parsing, and no input validation beyond truthy checks. Security awareness is not yet part of any model's default behavior for code generation tasks.

The prompt is jailbreak-resistant by design, not by accident. Its technical/engineering framing avoids the pattern signatures that identity-replacement jailbreaks target. This is a structural advantage over ethics-based safety approaches.

Model capability amplifies prompt effectiveness. More capable models extracted deeper security value from the same 225-line prompt. Flash produced basic protections; Pro produced production-grade hardening. The prompt scales with the model.

Each model has a different strongest feature. Flash: delete confirmation. Thinking: strict equality, form semantics. Pro: schema validation, quota handling. GPT-5.2: visible error states, strict mode. No single model produces the "ideal" secure output. The prompt creates a floor, not a ceiling.

The prompt improves usability, not just security. GPT-5.2's unsecured output required a React toolchain; the secured output was a single runnable file. Supply chain security guidance had the side effect of eliminating framework dependencies.

