Understanding what your AI coding assistant should (and shouldn't) be doing
HOW TO USE THIS DOCUMENT
This guide is the plain-English companion to a system prompt designed to make AI code assistants generate more secure code. You don't need to be a developer to read it — every technical term is explained where it first appears.

There are two versions of this document:

This one (Human-Readable): For you to read, understand, and use as a reference when reviewing AI-generated code. Every section explains what a security risk is, why it matters, and how to check for it — in language that doesn't assume you can read code.
The LLM variant: A compressed version stripped of the explanations, designed to be pasted into a system prompt, CLAUDE.md, or custom instruction field. The AI reads that one; you read this one.
What you should do with this:

Read Sections 1 and 3 before your next AI coding session. These are the "what to check" sections.
When reviewing AI-generated code, use the checklists — you can check most items by asking the AI "does this code do [X]?" and comparing the answer to what this guide says it should do.
Keep the Section 3.1 "Five Questions" open during coding sessions. They're the minimum viable security review.
PREAMBLE — WHAT YOUR AI ASSISTANT SHOULD PRIORITIZE
The AI generating your code should be optimizing for "code that is safe to run" — not just "code that runs." When you paste the LLM variant of this prompt into your AI assistant's instructions, it tells the AI to follow these defaults:

Deny by default, allow explicitly. Nothing should be accessible, connectable, or permitted unless the code specifically says so.
Fail closed, not open. When something goes wrong — an error, a timeout, a missing value — the system should lock down, not open up. (Think of it like a door that locks itself when the power goes out, rather than swinging open.)
Trust nothing from outside. User input, browser headers, uploaded files, data from other services — all of it is hostile until the code has verified otherwise.
Expose nothing unnecessary. Passwords, API keys, internal error details, system paths — none of this should ever appear in anything a user or the network can see.
Assume the deployment environment is hostile until proven otherwise. If someone runs this with zero configuration, it should be as locked down as possible.
If you notice your AI assistant generating code that violates any of these, that's a red flag worth questioning.

WHO THIS IS FOR — AND WHY IT EXISTS
Researchers at UC San Diego identified "conversational programmers" — product managers, executives, marketers, and other non-developers who use AI to build working software without reading the code — as a distinct and growing user persona (Lau & Guo, 2025). Andrej Karpathy coined the term "vibe coding" for this approach: describing what you want in natural language and letting the AI write everything.

The tools work. The security doesn't come included. AI coding assistants produce vulnerable code in 45% of security-relevant scenarios (Veracode, 2025), and AI copilots introduce 322% more privilege escalation paths compared to manually-written code (Apiiro, 2025). When researchers tested five leading AI coding agents (Cursor, Claude Code, Codex, Replit, Devin) building identical apps, none of the 15 resulting applications had CSRF protection or security headers, and the test produced 69 vulnerabilities total (Tenzai, 2026). A passive scan of 5,600 publicly deployed vibe-coded apps found over 2,000 vulnerabilities, 400+ exposed secrets, and 175 instances of exposed personal data including medical records and financial information (Escape.tech, 2025). One platform — Lovable — was assigned CVE-2025-48757 (CVSS 9.3) after researchers found that missing database security policies let unauthenticated attackers read and write arbitrary user data across 170 tested applications.

Meanwhile, the workshops teaching people to use these tools don't mention security at all. The AI Learning Club's 13-page Google AI Studio & Gemini CLI workshop guide (VibewithSnehal, Feb 2026) walks participants through 9+ complete app builds — calculators, budget trackers, SaaS landing pages — with zero mentions of authentication, input validation, or secure defaults. The companion Claude Code Setup Guide (26 pages) includes a code-reviewer agent template that mentions security, but only as an optional add-on — not a default. In early 2026, a vibe-coded startup was exploited when API keys were left exposed in frontend code. 175 customers were charged $500 each before the keys could be rotated.

A separate study found that when users can see the security choices an AI is making, they produce 31% fewer vulnerabilities — even without security training (Anaya Gonzalez et al., 2025). The problem isn't that people don't care about security. It's that the tools don't surface the choices being made on their behalf.

This prompt exists to close that gap. It tells the AI to make secure choices by default and explain those choices out loud, so you can evaluate them even if you can't read the code.

SECTION 1: BEFORE YOU WRITE — THE SECURITY PREFLIGHT
Before generating any code that involves the following, STOP and explicitly address the security implications in your response. Do not silently make assumptions.

1.1 — Authentication & Access Control
This is the #1 vulnerability in production software as of 2025 (OWASP A01). GitHub's own data shows that LLMs are statistically worse at this than any other security category — AI-generated code tends to create endpoints that look correct but have no actual login checks.

Before writing any endpoint, route, or API handler (an endpoint is a URL your server responds to, like /api/settings — each one is a door into your system), answer:

 Who is allowed to call this? Authenticated users only? Admins only? Anyone? Spell it out.
 How is that enforced? Is there a security checkpoint that every request passes through before reaching this code? (This is called "middleware" — think of it like a TSA checkpoint before the gate.) Is there a check attached directly to this specific function? (Called a "decorator" — like a bouncer at one specific door.) Or is the check written inside the function itself? Whatever the method — where exactly is the check, and what happens if it's accidentally removed?
 If there's a list of routes that DON'T require login (an "allowlist"), does it use exact matching — not substring, not prefix, not suffix? (Huntarr's auth code used '/api/user/2fa/' in request.path — substring matching — which exempted far more endpoints than intended. The string /api/user/2fa/ appears inside /api/user/2fa/admin-secret-panel too.)
 Does ANY endpoint return sensitive data in its response? API keys, passwords, tokens, internal configuration? If yes, are those fields filtered out before the response is sent? Only return what the caller actually needs.
 If there is a "setup mode" or "first-run" state (common in self-hosted apps — the initial configuration wizard before an admin account exists), is it controlled by the server's own internal state — NOT by a flag the client sends? (Huntarr let the client send {"setup_mode": true} and the server believed it, skipping all login checks. The client should never be able to tell the server what mode it's in.)
 Are admin operations (reset, clear, wipe, unlink, delete, reconfigure) behind authenticated sessions with appropriate privilege levels? (Huntarr's /api/setup/clear endpoint — which wiped the entire setup and let anyone re-register as the owner — required zero authentication.)
The Huntarr Test: Imagine sending a single request to any of your endpoints with no login cookie, no API key, no token — nothing. Does anything succeed that shouldn't? Does any response contain credentials? If you can't confidently say no, you have a broken access control vulnerability.

1.2 — Configuration & Defaults
OWASP A02 — Security Misconfiguration. This jumped from #5 to #2 in 2025. Every tested application showed some form of misconfiguration.

 What address does this listen on? If the answer is 0.0.0.0, justify why. 0.0.0.0 means "accept connections from everywhere — this machine, the local network, and the entire internet if there's no firewall." The default should be 127.0.0.1, which means "only accept connections from this machine itself." (OpenClaw defaulted to 0.0.0.0, which is why 135,000+ instances ended up exposed to the internet with zero protection.)
 Are there default credentials? If yes, does the application force the user to change them on first use? Defaults like admin/admin are the first thing attackers try.
 Are debug and development modes off by default? Debug modes often grant extra access and verbose logging (recording everything the system does in detail, including potentially sensitive data like passwords and API keys passing through the system). These should never be on in production.
 Does the Docker container or deployment run as root? Root is "god mode" on Linux — full access to every file and process on the system, similar to rooting a phone. If your application gets compromised while running as root, the attacker owns the entire machine. The default should be a non-root user with limited permissions. (Huntarr ran as root — PUID=0 — which meant the zip vulnerability could write files anywhere on the system.)
 Are config files and environment variables excluded from version control (not committed to Git) and not written to logs?
The OpenClaw Test: If someone deploys this with zero configuration changes — just runs the default — what is exposed to the network? The answer should be "nothing" or "as little as possible."

1.3 — Input Trust Boundaries
OWASP A05 (Injection) and A10 (Mishandling Exceptional Conditions). Every piece of data entering your system from outside is hostile until validated.

 User form input — validated (is it the right type? the right length? within expected values?) and sanitized (are dangerous characters removed or escaped?).
 URL parameters and query strings — same treatment.
 HTTP headers — these are pieces of metadata your browser sends along with every request, like "here's my IP address" or "here's what page I came from." The critical thing is: the person sending the request controls these headers. They can put anything they want in them. Headers like X-Forwarded-For (which claims to carry the user's real IP address) must never be trusted for security decisions unless you have a properly configured proxy that overwrites them. (Huntarr trusted X-Forwarded-For to decide if someone was "local" and could skip login. An attacker anywhere in the world could add X-Forwarded-For: 127.0.0.1 to pretend they were on the local machine.)
 File uploads — filenames must be sanitized to remove path traversal sequences like ../. On a computer, ../ means "go up one directory." A file named ../../../etc/cron.d/backdoor doesn't save where you expect — it walks up the directory tree and writes to the system's scheduled-task folder, potentially giving the attacker persistent code execution. Also: validate file types, enforce size limits, and extract to sandboxed directories.
 API responses from external services — validate before using. Don't assume they're well-formed just because they came from a "trusted" source.
 Environment variables — validate at startup. If required values are missing, fail immediately with a clear error, don't silently continue with empty values.
 Deserialized data — Serialization is packing data for transport; deserialization is unpacking it. Some formats like JSON are safe — they can only contain simple data (text, numbers, lists). But formats like Python's pickle can contain executable instructions — the package literally tells the computer "run this code when you unpack me." Python's own documentation warns: "The pickle module is not secure. Only unpickle data you trust." If you let untrusted users send data in formats like pickle, YAML (without safe_load), or Java's native serialization, they can execute arbitrary code on your server. Always use safe formats (JSON) or safe parsing modes. After parsing, validate the structure — check that the result has the expected types, fields, and lengths, not just that parsing succeeded. Data stored in the browser's localStorage, for example, can be edited by other scripts on the same domain or by the user directly through developer tools. Valid JSON with wrong field types or unexpected values can still break your application or cause security failures.
The Huntarr Test: Could a user-supplied value become part of a file path? (Huntarr's backup_id went straight into filesystem operations, enabling directory deletion.) Could a user-uploaded zip write outside its intended directory? (Huntarr used extractall() with no path checking.) Could a client-supplied flag bypass server-side checks? (Huntarr's setup_mode: true.)

1.4 — Secrets & Credential Handling
OWASP A04 — Cryptographic Failures.

 Are passwords, API keys, or tokens stored in code or config files? Secrets should never be hardcoded in source code, especially code that might be shared or committed to Git. Where they should live, ranked from safest to least safe:
OS keychain or credential manager (macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, Linux Secret Service) — encrypted, access-controlled, sometimes biometric-locked.
Secret references — pointers to secrets stored in a vault (like op://vault/item/field for 1Password, or AWS Secrets Manager ARNs). The pointer is safe to commit; the secret never touches disk.
Environment variables — acceptable for CI/CD and containers. Parse them once at startup, then remove from the process environment so child processes don't inherit them. Note: docker inspect exposes all environment variables, so this is not bulletproof in containers.
Config files with strict permissions (0600 — owner read/write only) — acceptable as a fallback. Credential files and non-secret config should be separate files (like AWS does with ~/.aws/config vs ~/.aws/credentials).
Command-line arguments — NEVER. Arguments are visible to every user on the system via ps aux, recorded in shell history, and captured by audit logs. This is not theoretical: CVE-2022-45868 (H2 Database) and CVE-2023-36052 (Azure CLI, CVSS 8.6) were both real-world exploits of secrets passed as CLI arguments.
 Do API responses include secrets? They should not. Return only what the caller needs. Never include API keys, passwords, or tokens in responses. (Huntarr returned the entire configuration tree — including every connected app's API keys in cleartext — in response to a simple settings change. Writing {"timezone": "UTC"} gave you back every secret in the system.)
 Are secrets logged anywhere? Check error messages, debug output, HTTP responses, and stack traces (a stack trace is the detailed error report showing the chain of code that led to a crash — useful for debugging but dangerous if exposed, because it can reveal file paths, database types, internal server addresses, and other information attackers use to plan further attacks). Secrets must never appear in any of these.
 Is HTTPS enforced for all communication involving credentials? HTTP is unencrypted — anyone between you and the server can read everything, like a postcard. HTTPS wraps it in TLS encryption — like a sealed envelope. TLS (Transport Layer Security) is the actual technology that makes the "S" in HTTPS work. Any time passwords, tokens, or keys are transmitted, it must be over HTTPS.
 Are passwords hashed with a modern algorithm? Use Argon2id (the current gold standard — winner of the 2015 Password Hashing Competition, recommended first by OWASP), scrypt, or bcrypt. Never use MD5, SHA-1, or SHA-256 alone for passwords. The reason: MD5 and SHA-1 are designed to be fast — modern GPUs can try 180+ billion MD5 hashes per second. An 8-character password hashed with MD5 can be cracked in minutes for about $1. The same password protected by Argon2 would cost $50,000+ and take years, because Argon2 is deliberately slow and memory-intensive. MD5 and SHA-1 also have known cryptographic weaknesses (collision attacks) beyond just speed. And never store passwords in plaintext — ever.
1.5 — Dependency & Supply Chain Awareness
OWASP A03 — Software Supply Chain Failures. This is a new Top 10 entry for 2025, with 50% of OWASP survey respondents ranking it their #1 concern.

 Are you recommending third-party packages? If so, are they well-known and actively maintained? Check for recent commits and a healthy issue tracker. Be especially vigilant for hallucinated packages — AI models sometimes suggest libraries that don't exist. Attackers have begun registering these hallucinated names with malicious code (called "slopsquatting"). If a package name looks unfamiliar, verify it exists on the official registry (npmjs.com, pypi.org) before installing.
 Watch for typosquatting — malicious packages with names one character off from popular ones. Real examples that contained actual malware: crossenv (typo of cross-env, stole environment variables), babelcli (typo of babel-cli, executed malicious code), mongose (typo of mongoose, exfiltrated data). Copy-paste package names from official documentation rather than typing them.
 Are dependencies pinned to specific versions? pip install flask==3.1.0 is safer than pip install flask, because the latter grabs whatever "latest" is — which could change at any time, including being compromised. Same logic for npm, cargo, etc. Research consistently finds that AI models suggest outdated or vulnerable dependencies — Veracode (2025) found 45% of AI-generated code in security-relevant contexts contained CWE vulnerabilities, with outdated dependencies a recurring factor. Always verify that suggested versions are current.
 Always commit your lockfile (package-lock.json, poetry.lock, Cargo.lock). Lockfiles record the exact versions installed, ensuring reproducible builds and making it harder for a compromised dependency to silently replace a good one.
 For CI/CD workflows (automated build and deploy scripts), are actions pinned to commit SHAs, not just version tags? A version tag like actions/checkout@v4 is a moving target — the owner can change what v4 points to at any time. If their account is compromised, every project using @v4 runs the attacker's code on the next build. A commit SHA (a unique fingerprint of a specific code version — like actions/checkout@a5ac7e51b41094c92402da3b24376905380afc29) points to one exact, immutable version that can't be changed after the fact.
 If the project has a plugin or extension system, is there any sandboxing, review, or trust boundary for third-party code? (OpenClaw's skills marketplace had 230+ malicious plugins published in a single week, downloaded thousands of times, because there was no moderation or security scanning.)
 If using MCP servers, Claude Code skills, or similar AI tool plugins, apply extra caution. These run with the AI agent's full permissions — they can read files, execute commands, and access secrets on your machine. Security research (Snyk, 2026) found 76 malicious payloads in public MCP server registries. Before installing any third-party MCP server or skill:
Is the source code available and readable?
Is the author/organization verified?
Does it request network access or file system access it shouldn't need?
Does it read sensitive files (.env, credentials, SSH keys)?
Is the version pinned (not latest)?
Important limitation: text-based instructions (like CLAUDE.md files or system prompts — including this one) are suggestions that can be overridden or ignored under pressure. If you need to enforce security constraints in an AI coding tool, use hooks or programmatic controls, not just written rules. Written rules are the seatbelt. Hooks are the airbag.
1.6 — Error Handling & Failure Modes
OWASP A10 — Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions. New category for 2025. 50% of OWASP survey respondents ranked this their #1 emerging concern. Applications that break unsafely when stressed are exploitable.

 When an error occurs, does the system fail closed (lock down) or fail open (allow access)? It must fail closed. If the authentication check crashes, the answer is "access denied" — not "sure, come in." (Think: a security door that locks when power is cut, not one that swings open.)
 Do error messages returned to users contain internal details? Stack traces (the detailed chain of code calls that led to the error), file paths, database queries, server addresses, or secrets must never be shown to users. Log the details internally for debugging; return a generic "something went wrong" message externally.
 Are null/undefined/None values handled explicitly? These are "nothing" values that can cause silent failures or crashes if code doesn't account for them.
 Are network timeouts, resource exhaustion, and unexpected input types handled with catch-and-deny logic? "Catch" means the code recognizes the unexpected situation instead of crashing. "Deny" means when something unexpected happens, the default response is "no" rather than "continue anyway." Together: recognize the problem, deny access, log what happened.
 Are storage and database writes protected, not just reads? Code that saves data to the browser (localStorage), a database, or the file system can fail — the storage might be full, the user might have revoked permission, or the disk might be out of space. If a write fails silently, the user thinks their data was saved when it wasn't. Wrap writes in error handling and tell the user when a save fails.
SECTION 2: WHILE YOU WRITE — ANTI-CHURN PATTERNS
These patterns address the common "paste error, get fix, paste new error" cycle that vibe coders experience. The goal is to produce code that works correctly on first attempt, or fails with clear, actionable error messages rather than cryptic crash reports. This directly improves security: a user exhausted from 45 minutes of error-pasting is more likely to accept the first solution that runs — whether or not it's secure.

2.1 — Produce Runnable Code
Include all imports/requires at the top. Do not assume the user knows what needs to be imported. A missing import creates an immediate error that the user can't diagnose.
Specify exact package versions in install commands. pip install flask==3.1.0 is better than pip install flask. This prevents version mismatches that cause confusing errors.
Include the full file, not snippets, when the user is building from scratch. Snippets cause integration errors (missing variables, broken references) that non-coders cannot debug without another round of error-pasting.
If code depends on prior code, say so explicitly. "This requires the auth_middleware function from the previous step" — don't leave the user guessing why something is undefined.
2.2 — Fail Clearly
Validate prerequisites at the top of scripts. Check for required environment variables, files, and dependencies before doing work. Print a human-readable message if something is missing — "Error: DATABASE_URL environment variable is not set. Please set it in your .env file." — not an incomprehensible traceback.
Use specific error types, not catch-all handlers. Catching every possible error silently (except: in Python, bare catch(e) in JS) hides problems and is a security risk — it can cause the program to continue running in a broken state rather than stopping.
Include type hints / type annotations where the language supports them. These tell the code (and the user's editor) what kind of data each variable should hold, catching mismatches before the code even runs.
Name variables descriptively. x, data, result create confusion when errors reference them. user_session, raw_api_response, sanitized_filename tell the user (and the AI in future debugging sessions) exactly what they're looking at.
2.3 — Explain What Might Break
When code interacts with external systems (APIs, databases, file systems, networks), include a brief comment about what can go wrong and how the code handles it.
If a pattern you're using has a known trap — a feature that looks safe but behaves dangerously in certain cases (e.g., Python's os.path.join silently accepting absolute paths and ignoring everything before them, JavaScript's == doing type coercion where "0" == false is true), warn about it in a comment.
If there are platform differences (Windows vs. Linux paths, macOS keychain behavior), mention them proactively.
2.4 — Structure for Modification
Separate configuration from logic. Hardcoded values (ports, URLs, timeouts) should be at the top of the file or in a config section, not buried inside functions.
One function, one job. Large functions that do many things are where security bugs hide and where non-coders get lost trying to understand the flow.
Make security checks visible. Authentication and authorization checks should be obvious — named functions like require_auth() or check_admin_permission(), not one-line conditionals buried inside business logic. If a security check is invisible, it's easy to accidentally delete.
SECTION 3: AFTER YOU WRITE — THE SECURITY REVIEW CHECKLIST
Before presenting code to the user, run through this mentally:

3.1 — The Five Questions
What does this expose to the network? List every port and endpoint. For each one: who can reach it (localhost only, or the whole internet?), and what can they do?
What happens with no authentication? Mentally trace every request path as someone with no login. Does anything succeed that shouldn't?
What secrets exist in this system? Where are they stored, who can read them, and do they ever appear in logs, error messages, or API responses?
What can user-controlled input touch? File paths? Database queries? Shell commands? HTML output? System configuration?
What happens when something fails? Does the system deny access, expose internal details, or silently continue in a broken state?
3.2 — Common LLM Blind Spots
These are patterns that AI code assistants — including you — tend to get wrong. Check for them explicitly:

Auth bypass through allowlist patterns. LLMs tend to check whether a URL contains a certain piece of text (called "substring matching" — checking if a short string appears anywhere inside a longer string) or starts with certain text (called "prefix matching" — checking only the beginning). Both are dangerous. if '/api/public' in path is a substring check — it asks "does the text /api/public appear anywhere in this URL?" The answer is yes for /api/public/info (intended) but also yes for /api/public-admin/delete-everything (definitely not intended). Prefix matching (path.startswith('/api/public')) has the same problem — /api/public-admin starts with /api/public too. Use exact route matching (the URL must be exactly /api/public, nothing more) or a framework's built-in route-level auth.
Returning too much data. LLMs tend to return full database objects or entire config stores rather than selecting only the fields needed. This is exactly how Huntarr's settings endpoint leaked every API key in the system.
Trusting client-side state. LLMs generate code that accepts client-supplied flags like is_admin: true or setup_mode: true rather than determining these values server-side. Never trust the client to tell you who they are or what state the system is in.
Reaching for dangerous functions. LLMs reach for these when safer alternatives exist:
os.system() — takes a text string and hands it to the operating system's command line to run as-is. If any part of that string came from a user, the user can inject additional commands. (Like handing someone a blank check with your signature already on it.)
subprocess with shell=True — same problem as os.system(). The shell=True flag means "interpret this string through the command line" instead of running the program directly.
eval() — takes a text string and executes it as code in the current programming language. If a user can control any part of that string, they can run any code they want on your server.
exec() — same as eval() but can execute multiple statements, not just a single expression. All of these allow arbitrary command or code execution if any user-controlled value gets into them.
Hardcoding secrets in source code — including in "example" code that gets copy-pasted into production without changes.
Missing or permissive CORS configuration. CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) controls which websites can make requests to your server. Setting it to * (allow all origins) by default means any website in the world can make authenticated requests to your API.
Generating Docker configs that run as root without a USER directive changing to a limited-permission user.
Using extractall() on zip/tar files without path validation. This is the Zip Slip vulnerability — files in the archive can contain ../ sequences that write outside the intended directory. Always validate that every extracted filename resolves to a path inside the target directory.
Accepting file paths from user input without sanitization. When code uses a value from the user to decide which file to open, read, or delete, the user can manipulate that value to access files outside the intended folder. Here's how: on every computer, ../ in a file path means "go up one directory." If your app serves files from /app/uploads/ and accepts a filename from the user, a request for ../../../etc/passwd doesn't look in the uploads folder — it walks up three directories and reads the system password file. This is called a "path traversal attack." The fix: after combining the base directory with the user's input, check that the final resolved path still starts with the base directory. If it doesn't, reject the request.
Defaulting network listeners to 0.0.0.0 (accept connections from everywhere) instead of 127.0.0.1 (this machine only). This is the single configuration choice that exposed 135,000+ OpenClaw instances to the internet.
SECTION 4: LANGUAGE-SPECIFIC SECURITY NOTES
You don't need to memorize these. They're here so you (or the AI) can check whether generated code contains known-dangerous patterns for the specific language being used. Each entry focuses on the mistakes AI assistants make most often in that language.

4.1 — Python
Dangerous functions AI reaches for:

Don't use	Use instead	Why
eval(user_input) or exec(user_input)	Parse with json.loads() or ast.literal_eval()	eval and exec execute arbitrary Python code. If any user data reaches them, an attacker has full control of your server. ast.literal_eval only parses simple data types (strings, numbers, lists) — no code execution.
os.system("rm " + filename)	os.remove(path) or subprocess.run(["rm", filename])	os.system() passes the string to a shell, which interprets special characters. A filename containing ; rm -rf / executes that as a separate command. The safe version passes arguments as a list, so they're never interpreted as commands.
subprocess.run(cmd, shell=True)	subprocess.run(["cmd", "arg1", "arg2"])	shell=True means the string is interpreted by the shell, enabling injection. The list form prevents this.
cursor.execute(f"...{user_input}...")	cursor.execute("... %s ...", (user_input,))	String formatting puts user data directly into SQL. Parameterized queries send data and SQL separately, making injection impossible.
pickle.loads(untrusted_data)	json.loads(untrusted_data)	Pickle can execute arbitrary code during deserialization. JSON cannot. Python's own docs warn against unpickling untrusted data.
yaml.load(data)	yaml.safe_load(data)	yaml.load can execute arbitrary Python code. safe_load only parses simple data types.
random.random() for tokens/passwords	secrets.token_urlsafe()	random is predictable — an attacker who sees a few outputs can predict future ones. secrets uses cryptographically secure randomness.
hashlib.md5(password)	argon2.PasswordHasher().hash(password)	MD5 is crackable in minutes for ~$1. Argon2 costs $50,000+ to crack. (See Section 1.4.)
os.system() or eval() with ANY user data	See first three rows	These are the two most common AI-generated vulnerabilities in Python. If you see either one in generated code and user data can reach it, stop and replace.
Python-specific gotcha: os.path.join("/safe/dir", user_input) looks safe but isn't — if user_input is an absolute path like /etc/passwd, Python silently discards the first argument and uses only the absolute path. Always validate that the final resolved path starts with your intended directory using os.path.commonpath() or pathlib.Path.resolve().

Validation: Use Pydantic for data validation. It enforces types, lengths, and patterns at the boundary where data enters your system. AI assistants know Pydantic well and will generate correct validation code if you ask for it.

4.2 — JavaScript / TypeScript
Dangerous functions AI reaches for:

Don't use	Use instead	Why
eval(userInput)	JSON.parse(userInput)	Same as Python's eval — executes arbitrary code.
element.innerHTML = userInput	element.textContent = userInput	innerHTML parses the string as HTML, including <script> tags. textContent treats it as plain text — no execution.
child_process.exec(cmd)	child_process.execFile(cmd, args)	exec passes through a shell (injection risk). execFile runs the command directly with arguments as an array.
document.write(data)	DOM manipulation methods	document.write can inject arbitrary HTML including scripts.
new Function(userString)	Structured parsing	Same as eval — creates and executes a function from a string.
== for security comparisons	===	== does "type coercion" — JavaScript silently converts values to matching types before comparing them, which produces surprising results. For example, the text "0" and the boolean false are completely different things, but "0" == false returns true because JavaScript converts them both to numbers first (0 equals 0). An empty string "" compared to false also returns true. In login or permission checks, this can cause the code to treat invalid values as valid. === (triple equals) checks both the type and the value — no silent conversion — so "0" === false correctly returns false.
Date.now() or Math.random() for unique IDs	crypto.randomUUID()	Date.now() returns the current time in milliseconds — if two items are created within the same millisecond, they get the same ID. Math.random() is not cryptographically random and can produce collisions. crypto.randomUUID() generates a properly unique identifier using the browser's built-in cryptographic random number generator.
eval() or innerHTML with ANY user data	See first two rows	These are the two most common AI-generated vulnerabilities in JavaScript. If you see either one in generated code and user data can reach it, stop and replace.
React-specific: React auto-escapes content by default — {userInput} in JSX is safe. The danger is dangerouslySetInnerHTML, which bypasses this protection entirely. If you must render user-supplied HTML, sanitize it first with DOMPurify: DOMPurify.sanitize(html). AI assistants sometimes reach for dangerouslySetInnerHTML when textContent would suffice.

npm-specific: Run npm audit after every install. Use package-lock.json (commit it to Git). For TypeScript projects, Zod provides runtime validation similar to Python's Pydantic — it catches bad data at the boundary.

4.3 — Shell / Bash
This is the most dangerous language for AI-generated code. Every variable expansion is a potential injection point, and there are no guardrails.

Don't do	Do instead	Why
rm -rf $DIR (unquoted)	rm -rf "$DIR" (quoted)	Unquoted variables undergo word splitting. If DIR contains spaces or glob characters, the command does something different than intended.
result=`command $user_input`	result=$(command "$user_input") with validation	Backtick substitution plus unquoted variables enables injection. But even quoted, validate first.
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" ...	Pass the env var name to the tool, let it read internally	Shell expansion puts $TOKEN into curl's argv, visible via ps aux to every user on the system. (See Section 1.4 on CLI arguments.)
echo "$PASSWORD" | command	command --password-file <(echo -n "$PASSWORD") or read from stdin	Piping is safer than argv, but process substitution (<(...)) is better — it creates a temporary file descriptor that never touches disk.
Shell-specific gotchas:

rm -rf requires explicit confirmation every time. Before generating or executing any rm -rf command (or rm -r, or any recursive delete), you MUST: (1) state in plain English exactly what directory or files will be deleted and why, (2) show the exact command as it will be executed with all variables resolved, and (3) wait for the user to confirm before running it. This applies regardless of how small or "obvious" the deletion seems. There is no "routine" recursive delete. A misquoted variable, an unresolved path, or a trailing space can turn rm -rf "$BUILD_DIR" into rm -rf /  (delete everything on the machine). This has happened to real people enough times to fill a subreddit.
Always quote variables — "$var", not $var. Unquoted variables are the #1 source of shell bugs and vulnerabilities.
Use set -euo pipefail at the top of every script. -e stops on errors (instead of silently continuing), -u catches undefined variables, -o pipefail catches failures in piped commands. Without these, a script can silently fail halfway through and continue executing in a broken state.
Never use eval with user-controlled data. Shell eval is even more dangerous than Python's eval because shell has direct access to the filesystem and can execute any system command.
Credential files should be created with permissions set atomically — install -m 600 /dev/null "$cred_file" then write, not touch "$cred_file" && chmod 600 "$cred_file" (the file briefly exists with default permissions in between).
4.4 — For Other Languages
If you're working in Go, Java, C#, PHP, Ruby, Rust, C/C++, or another language not covered above, the principles from Sections 1–3 still apply. Ask the AI assistant: "What are the known-dangerous functions in [language] for: command execution, SQL queries, file operations, HTML rendering, deserialization, and cryptographic randomness? What are the safe alternatives?" The answer should name specific functions — not just principles.

SECTION 5: LLM SELF-AWARENESS — KNOWN FAILURE PATTERNS
This section is addressed directly to the AI model generating code. If you are a human reading this, it's here so you understand the systematic weaknesses of the tool you're using — and so the AI itself can compensate for them when this prompt is in its context.

5.1 — Why You Generate Insecure Code
You optimize for "code that works" before "code that is safe." This is a training artifact, not a deliberate choice — your training data contains vastly more functional code than secure code. Independent research quantifies the problem: 45% of AI-generated code in security-relevant scenarios contains vulnerabilities (Veracode, 2025), AI copilots introduce 322% more privilege escalation paths and 153% more architectural design flaws than manually-written code (Apiiro, 2025), and when AI agents were given 200 real-world feature requests from open-source projects, 61% of solutions were functionally correct but only 10.5% were secure (Zhao et al., 2025). Users accept your insecure defaults because the model's most confident answer looks like the correct one — even when safer alternatives exist (Anaya Gonzalez et al., 2025). The result is predictable:

You prioritize the shortest path to working. If the prompt doesn't explicitly demand security, you produce the simplest solution — even if it uses eval(), concatenates SQL strings, or hardcodes credentials. This is because your training reward was primarily "does this answer the question" and "does this look like correct code," not "would this survive a security audit."

You learned from insecure examples. A significant fraction of publicly available code — Stack Overflow answers, tutorials, blog posts, GitHub repos — contains bad practices. Tutorials especially prioritize clarity over security, and you absorbed those priorities. When you generate a login form, you're pattern-matching against thousands of tutorials that skipped input validation because it wasn't the point of the lesson.

You don't know the deployment context. You don't know whether this code runs on a laptop for a class project or on a public-facing server handling payment data. You don't know the threat model, compliance requirements, or who has network access. In the absence of context, you fill gaps with assumptions — and those assumptions are almost always "this is a simple example" rather than "this will be attacked."

Your dependency knowledge is stale. Your training data has a cutoff. You may recommend package versions with known vulnerabilities because those versions were current when you learned about them. Always verify that the versions you suggest are current and unaffected by known CVEs.

You hallucinate packages. You sometimes suggest libraries that don't exist — names that sound plausible but have no corresponding real package. Attackers monitor for these hallucinated names and register them with malicious code ("slopsquatting"). If you suggest a package, verify it's real before the user installs it.

5.2 — Your Specific Weak Spots
These are the categories where you fail most often, based on security research analyzing AI-generated code. Check your output against each one:

Authentication & access control — This is your worst category. You generate endpoints that handle business logic correctly but have no auth checks. You create admin panels without permission verification. You write route allowlists using substring matching. You generate "setup mode" flags that the client can set. When you do include auth, you tend to check authentication (who are you?) but not authorization (are you allowed to do this specific thing?).

Returning too much data — You return full database objects, complete config stores, and entire API responses rather than selecting only the fields needed. This is how secrets leak. When you write a user profile endpoint, your instinct is return user rather than return {name: user.name, email: user.email}.

Secrets in source code — You hardcode API keys, database passwords, and JWT secrets directly in code. You do this even in code labeled "production-ready." You generate .env.example files with real-looking secrets that users copy without changing. Your examples use SECRET_KEY = "my-secret-key" and the user never changes it.

Default-open configurations — You default to 0.0.0.0 (listen everywhere), CORS: * (allow all origins), debug: true, and root user execution. Every one of these should be the opposite by default.

Unsafe deserialization and command execution — You reach for pickle, eval, exec, os.system, shell=True, and yaml.load when safer alternatives exist. You do this because these are the patterns you've seen most often in training data, and they produce the shortest code.

File operation vulnerabilities — You use extractall() without path validation, os.path.join() without checking the result, and accept user-supplied filenames without sanitization. These are the exact patterns behind Huntarr's zip exploit and path traversal vulnerabilities.

Missing error handling on security paths — You add try/catch blocks to business logic but forget them on authentication and authorization code. When auth code crashes with an unhandled exception, the request often continues as if auth succeeded (fail-open).

5.3 — What To Do About It
When this prompt is in your context, apply these compensating behaviors:

Assume hostile deployment. Unless the user explicitly says "this is a local-only prototype," generate code as if it will face the public internet.
Add auth checks even if the prompt doesn't ask for them. If you're generating an API endpoint, include authentication. If the user didn't want it, they can remove it — but they can't add what they don't know is missing.
Return minimal data. Select specific fields rather than returning full objects. If you're unsure what's needed, return less and explain what else is available.
Use safe defaults. 127.0.0.1, not 0.0.0.0. CORS restricted to specific origins, not *. Debug off. Non-root user. Secrets in environment variables, not source code.
Prefer safe functions. Parameterized queries over string concatenation. subprocess.run() with a list over os.system(). json.loads() over eval(). Every time, without exception.
State your assumptions out loud. If you're making a security decision (binding address, auth model, secret storage), say so in a comment or explanation so the user can evaluate it. Silent assumptions are where vulnerabilities hide.
Confirm before deleting, even in client-only apps. Apply confirmation prompts to any user-initiated deletion — not just server-side destructive operations. Client-only apps get deployed to servers without anyone revisiting the delete logic. The habit tracker that doesn't confirm today becomes the SaaS product that silently wipes user data tomorrow.
Verify your own package suggestions. If you're recommending a dependency and you're not certain it exists or is current, say so. "I believe this package exists, but verify on [registry] before installing" is better than confidently suggesting a hallucinated or outdated package.
