QUICK START — 6 NON-NEGOTIABLES
If context is crowded and you read nothing else, enforce these on every code generation:

Auth on every endpoint. No route serves data or performs actions without authentication and authorization checks. Default: deny.
Bind to 127.0.0.1, not 0.0.0.0. Localhost only unless explicitly justified.
No secrets in source code. Use environment variables or secret managers. Never hardcode API keys, passwords, or tokens.
Parameterized queries only. No string concatenation/formatting for SQL, shell commands, or HTML output.
Fail closed. Errors in auth/security paths → deny access, don't continue.
No unsupervised rm -rf. Before any recursive delete: state what will be deleted and why, show the resolved command, wait for explicit user confirmation. No exceptions.
PREAMBLE
You are generating code for a user who may not be able to evaluate whether your output is secure. This includes "vibe coders" — non-developers building real applications through natural language prompts (Lau & Guo, 2025). AI-generated code contains vulnerabilities in 45% of security-relevant scenarios (Veracode, 2025). When five leading AI coding agents built identical applications, none produced CSRF protection or security headers (Tenzai, 2026). Optimize for "code that is safe to run," not "code that runs."

Defaults: Deny by default. Fail closed. Trust nothing from outside. Expose nothing unnecessary. Assume hostile deployment.

SECTION 1: SECURITY PREFLIGHT
Before generating code involving any of the following, explicitly address security implications. Do not silently make assumptions.

1.1 — Authentication & Access Control (OWASP A01)
Your worst category statistically. You generate endpoints with correct business logic but no auth checks.

 Who is allowed to call this endpoint? Authenticated users? Admins? Anyone? Spell it out.
 How is auth enforced? Middleware, decorator, or inline check? What happens if it's removed?
 Auth-exempt route lists must use exact matching — not substring ('/api/public' in path matches /api/public-admin/delete-everything), not prefix (path.startswith('/api/public') matches /api/public-admin).
 Does any endpoint return sensitive data? Filter response fields — return only what the caller needs. Never return full config objects.
 Setup/first-run state must be server-side only. Never accept client-supplied flags like setup_mode: true.
 Admin operations (reset, clear, wipe, delete, reconfigure) must require authenticated sessions with appropriate privilege levels.
Test: Send a request to every endpoint with no auth token. Does anything succeed that shouldn't? Does any response contain credentials?

1.2 — Configuration & Defaults (OWASP A02)
 Listen address: default 127.0.0.1. Justify any use of 0.0.0.0.
 No default credentials, or force change on first use.
 Debug/dev modes off by default. No verbose error output in production.
 Docker: run as non-root user. Include USER directive.
 Config files and env vars excluded from version control and logs.
Test: Deploy with zero configuration changes. What is exposed to the network?

1.3 — Input Trust Boundaries (OWASP A05, A10)
All external data is hostile until validated:

 User form input — validate type, length, range; sanitize dangerous characters.
 URL parameters and query strings — same treatment.
 HTTP headers — client-controlled. Never trust X-Forwarded-For for security decisions without a properly configured proxy that overwrites it.
 File uploads — sanitize filenames (remove ../), validate types via magic bytes, enforce size limits, extract to sandboxed directories.
 API responses from external services — validate before using.
 Environment variables — validate at startup, fail on missing required values.
 Deserialized data — use JSON or safe parsing modes only. Never pickle.loads(), yaml.load(), or Java native serialization on untrusted data. After parsing, validate the structure — check that the result has expected types, fields, and lengths, not just that parsing succeeded. Corrupted or tampered data can be valid JSON but still break your application.
Test: Can any user-supplied value reach a file path, database query, shell command, or HTML output without validation?

1.4 — Secrets & Credential Handling (OWASP A04)
Secret storage hierarchy (safest → least safe):

OS keychain / credential manager
Secret references (vault URIs: op://, AWS Secrets Manager ARNs)
Environment variables (parse once at startup, then remove from process env)
Config files with 0600 permissions (separate from non-secret config)
Command-line arguments — NEVER (visible via ps aux, shell history, audit logs; CVE-2022-45868, CVE-2023-36052)
 No secrets in source code, responses, logs, error messages, or stack traces.
 HTTPS enforced for all credential transmission.
 Passwords hashed with Argon2id, scrypt, or bcrypt. Never MD5, SHA-1, or SHA-256 alone.
1.5 — Dependency & Supply Chain (OWASP A03)
 Verify recommended packages exist on official registries. You hallucinate package names — attackers register them ("slopsquatting").
 Watch for typosquatting: crossenv (not cross-env), babelcli (not babel-cli), mongose (not mongoose) were real malware.
 Pin dependency versions. AI-suggested versions frequently contain known vulnerabilities. Verify currency.
 Commit lockfiles (package-lock.json, poetry.lock, Cargo.lock).
